  "description": "A 1-minute de-risking session on OCD: The Loop That Won't Stop.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "You locked the door. You know you did. But the urge to check again is unbearable. You check twenty times.",
      "visual": "🔒"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "OCD creates intrusive, unwanted thoughts (obsessions) and repetitive behaviors to reduce the anxiety (compulsions).",
      "visual": "🔁"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "The caudate nucleus, part of the basal ganglia, normally filters and suppresses irrelevant thoughts. In OCD it fails.",
      "visual": "🧠"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "A communication loop between the orbital frontal cortex and the caudate gets stuck—like a broken record.",
      "visual": "📀"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Exposure and response prevention therapy physically changes brain activity patterns in this circuit over time.",
      "visual": "🔧"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "OCD isn't about being tidy. It's a brain circuit stuck in an error loop.",
      "visual": "⚙️"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What brain structure is involved in filtering thoughts in OCD?",
    "options": [
      "The caudate nucleus",
      "The cerebellum",
      "The hippocampus"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
